    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.018.c2",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "Disaster at the Allia",
      "body": "In about 390 BCE, an army of Gauls led by a chief named Brennus crushed the Romans at the Allia River. The Gauls then marched into Rome and sacked it. Some sources date the sack to 387 BCE."
    },
    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.018.c3",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "Woe to the conquered",
      "body": "According to Livy, when the Romans protested the ransom the Gauls demanded, Brennus threw his sword onto the scales and said “Vae victis”: woe to the conquered. Legend adds that Juno’s sacred geese warned the defenders of the Capitoline of a night attack."
    },
    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.018.c4",
      "type": "fact",
      "fact": "Rome built a wall.",
      "context": "After the sack, Rome surrounded itself with a strong stone wall, later called the Servian Wall."
    },
